      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure I\u0027m enough of an expert on i2c to catch any bugs here, but really curious what was wrong with the old/tested algorithm from stm32f.  I can understand that it doesn\u0027t deal as well with clock stretching.  ...but it did have the nice property that it would try to avoid sending a bogus transaction while unwedging.\n\nSpecifically if we were midway through a write command it\u0027s nice to actually NAK the write rather than completing it.\n\nAlso: since you\u0027re driving sda low while you\u0027re clocking signals, won\u0027t that be interpreted as an ACK by the other side?  ...so it might start writing out the next byte?",
      "revId": "60ac7c78bc8d66e256fdac04212d85dcb7dcaae0",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAc3//zNQ\u003d",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c-stm32l.c",
